    "taxonId": 69135,
    "summary": "Chilli pepper native to the Americas, with a possible centre of origin in the tropical northern Amazon. Its varieties have distinctive flavours and range from non-spicy to exceptionally hot. Fruits vary greatly in shape and colour, commonly ripening red, orange or yellow.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Used for millennia in Yucatán and Caribbean cooking, especially in stews, sauces and marinades for meat and chicken.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p5", "s1:p7"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p12"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 126485,
    "summary": "Flowering shrub native to the eastern United States. Its solitary spring flowers are dark red to reddish purple and aromatic, while the leaves also release fragrance when bruised.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "The bark is reportedly edible and used as a cinnamon substitute; caution is advised because an alkaloid may cause heart convulsions.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p4", "s1:p8"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p9"]
    }
  },
